# The LSP framing case. `core.autocrlf = input` is set here, and its whole job
# is to make a carriage return in a working tree not survive a commit -- which
# is right for source and wrong for these two: LSP frames a message with
# `Content-Length: N<CR><LF><CR><LF>`, so the carriage returns *are* the
# subject. The input holds one frame written that way and one written with a
# bare `<LF>`, because the reader is lenient and the writer is not; the golden
# holds what the writer produced, which is the only thing that can say the
# writer is strict (ADR-0218).
tests/dialect/lib_lsp.in -text
tests/dialect/lib_lsp.out -text

# MCP's framing, and the carriage return is the subject for the opposite
# reason: that transport is one message to a *line*, so a `<CR>` before the
# newline belongs to neither the framing nor the message and the reader drops
# it. The input holds one message written CRLF for exactly that, and a commit
# that normalised it would leave the case passing with nothing to drop --
# reporting the same byte count for the wrong reason, which is worse than
# failing (ADR-0241).
tests/dialect/lib_lsp_jsonl.in -text

# The language server's session goldens, for the reason above and one more:
# what they hold is the exact bytes the server wrote, `Content-Length` header
# and all, so the carriage returns are the subject and the byte counts are part
# of what is pinned. A commit that rewrote them would move the counts too, and
# the frames would then disagree with the numbers announcing them (ADR-0236).
lsp/sessions/*.out -text
